<sn><md>### Basic Command Structure

```bash
rclone COMMAND REMOTE:PATH LOCAL_PATH
```

For example:

```bash
rclone copy mega:Backup ~/Documents
```

Here:

```text
mega:Backup ─────────────► ~/Documents
☁️ Cloud                    💻 Local

SOURCE                     DESTINATION
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Using `sync`

Suppose the cloud contains:

```text
☁️ CLOUD

📁 Backup
├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt
```

But your local folder contains an extra file:

```text
💻 LOCAL

📁 Documents
├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 Old.txt
```

Run:

```bash
rclone sync mega:Backup ~/Documents
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Synchronization Process

```text
☁️ CLOUD                         💻 LOCAL

📁 Backup                        📁 Documents
├── 📄 A.txt ──────────────────► 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt ──────────────────► 📄 B.txt
├── 📄 C.txt ──────────────────► 📄 C.txt
                                 ❌ Old.txt
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### After Synchronization

```text
☁️ CLOUD                         💻 LOCAL

📁 Backup                        📁 Documents
├── 📄 A.txt                     ├── 📄 A.txt
├── 📄 B.txt                     ├── 📄 B.txt
└── 📄 C.txt                     └── 📄 C.txt
```

The local destination now becomes identical to the cloud source.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Summary

| Operation | Direction | Cloud Source Files       | Extra Local Files |
| --------- | --------- | ------------------------ | ----------------- |
| `copy`    | ☁️ → 💻   | ✔️ Remain                | ✔️ Remain         |
| `move`    | ☁️ → 💻   | ❌ Removed after transfer | ✔️ Remain         |
| `sync`    | ☁️ → 💻   | ✔️ Remain                | ❌ Deleted         |

&gt; ⚠️ The order of source and destination is extremely important with `rclone sync`.

```bash
rclone sync mega:Backup ~/Documents
```

This means:

```text
☁️ mega:Backup
       │
       │ Source
       ▼
💻 ~/Documents
   Destination
```

Before performing an important sync, safely check the result with:

```bash
rclone sync mega:Backup ~/Documents --dry-run
```
